// heart - a closed silhouette made of two lobe arcs, a notch and a point, like bell and home are made of arcs and lines.
// The decision matters because a heart is judged almost entirely by its outline at 16: the proportion (how wide for how tall),
// whether the flanks run straight or swell, how deep the notch cuts and how sharp the point is are the whole character of the icon,
// and the filled twin has nothing else to show. Ten candidates move one of those at a time from a plain geometric base (A).
// Generated by a throwaway script (exact arcs and tangencies, no cubics - an arc offsets exactly into the filled outer edge, a cubic
// does not); every number in the notes was computed there from the same construction the paths were written from.
// Every notch is a C10 bend (half the weight on the line) unless the note says it is softened, so the outer edges meet in one point
// and no candidate leaves a sliver of air in the notch. Lint never measures a closed outline against itself; the notch numbers are the script's.

